import io.jsonwebtoken.Jwts;
import javax.crypto.SecretKey;
import java.util.Date;

SecretKey key = Jwts.SIG.HS256.key().build(); // demo: persist securely in a real service
Date now = new Date();

String token = Jwts.builder()
    .issuer("https://auth.example")
    .subject("user-42")
    .audience().add("orders-api").and()
    .issuedAt(now)
    .expiration(new Date(now.getTime() + 10 * 60_000L))
    .signWith(key)
    .compact();

var claims = Jwts.parser()
    .verifyWith(key)
    .build()
    .parseSignedClaims(token)
    .getPayload();
```
*Signature verification is only one check. A real verifier must also validate issuer, audience, time claims, and the expected algorithm for its trust boundary.*

```java
@Bean
JwtDecoder jwtDecoder() {
    NimbusJwtDecoder decoder = JwtDecoders.fromIssuerLocation("https://auth.example");
    OAuth2TokenValidator<Jwt> issuerAndTime =
        JwtValidators.createDefaultWithIssuer("https://auth.example");
    OAuth2TokenValidator<Jwt> audience = token ->
        token.getAudience().contains("orders-api")
            ? OAuth2TokenValidatorResult.success()
            : OAuth2TokenValidatorResult.failure(
                new OAuth2Error("invalid_token", "Wrong audience", null));
    decoder.setJwtValidator(new DelegatingOAuth2TokenValidator<>(issuerAndTime, audience));
    return decoder;
}
```
*The decoder verifies the issuer's keys and standard time claims; the added validator prevents a token for another API from being reused here.*

